# 0015. Vim keys for moving around

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-25
- Amends: [0008](0008-slash-commands-and-mentions.adr) (keys)

The owner prefers vim keys in every tool they build. Roundtable's keys are a mix
(`Ctrl-B`, `Ctrl-K`, `Tab` cycling, arrows in panes, `PgUp`/`PgDn`). This ADR gives the
panes vim navigation while typing stays plain, and keeps `PgUp`/`PgDn` as the owner asked.
No jump mode (easyjump-style labels): tmux copy mode covers that.

## Technical details
- **Two modes, shown in the status line** (`-- INSERT --` / `-- NORMAL --`):
  - Insert (start-up mode): the input box takes every printable key, as today. `Enter`
    sends, `Alt-Enter` new line, `Tab` completes. `Esc` goes to Normal.
  - Normal: keys move and act; nothing is typed. `i`, `a` or `/` (with `/` pre-typed) go
    back to Insert. `:` opens Insert with `/` pre-typed, so `:help` and `/help` both work.
- **Normal-mode keys**:
  | Key | Does |
  |---|---|
  | `j` / `k` | next / previous chat line (expandable lines first, as today's Up/Down) |
  | `Ctrl-D` / `Ctrl-U` | half a page down / up |
  | `gg` / `G` | top / bottom of the thread (`G` also leaves scroll-back) |
  | `Enter` or `o` | expand or collapse the selected line (ask, handoff, tool call) |
  | `h` / `l` | focus the pane to the left / right (threads, chat, context) |
  | `y` | copy the selected message to the clipboard (OSC 52, works over ssh and tmux) |
  | `q` | close the side panes; twice quits like `Ctrl-C` twice |
  | `x` | cancel running replies (as `Esc` does today) |
  | `?` | `/help` |
  In the threads pane `j`/`k` move and `Enter` resumes.
- **Unchanged**: `PgUp`/`PgDn` scroll in both modes; `Ctrl-C` twice quits; `Ctrl-D` on an
  empty input quits in Insert only (in Normal it is half a page); `Ctrl-B` and
  `Ctrl-K` still toggle the side panes (vim users rarely need them, nothing to unlearn).
- **Cancel moves**: `Esc` in Insert now switches to Normal. With replies running, the
  first `Esc` also cancels them as today, so muscle memory still works; the help text
  says so.
- **Implementation**: `App::mode: Mode { Insert, Normal }`, keys dispatched in
  `App::key` by mode; a pending `g` is state, cleared by any other key. Covered by unit
  tests per key and an insta snapshot of the status line in each mode.
- Consequences: the owner's vim habits work in the panes; Insert mode means typing is
  never captured by a motion. Costs: one more mode to show and explain; `Esc` gains a
  second meaning.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: crossterm key events, OSC 52 for `y` (no clipboard crate)
- Alternatives:
  - Modeless with `Ctrl-J`/`Ctrl-K`-style chords: no Insert/Normal split, but not vim.
  - A `vim = true` switch keeping both layouts: two key maps to test, for one user.
  - Vim editing inside the input box (motions, `ciw`): large; `/log` and a future
    "edit prompt in $EDITOR" give real vim for long text.
